Yes, there are several. I read GeneralizedBuchiAutomata, FormulaGraphFactory, Node, Atom and the interning in FormulaFactory, but I haven't profiled anything yet. The order below is my estimate of how much each would help.

1. Debug printing runs on every build

GeneralizedBuchiAutomata.translate() always calls printNodes(new StandardOutput()). That turns every node, atom and transition into strings and writes them out. On large automata this alone can take a big share of the time. Put it behind a debug flag.

2. FormulaFactory.unique() builds a key with toString()

String s = f.toString();
if (subf.containsKey(s)) ...
Every makeNot, makeNext, makePrev and makeRelease turns the whole formula tree into a string just to look it up. Several of these calls sit in hot paths:
- isContradiction calls makeNot(f) for every formula added in splitAtomToCoverSet.
- specialCaseV calls makeRelease(False, f) on every Atom.add redundancy check.
- getPreconditions calls makeNext(formula).
- LabelFactory.makeLabel calls fac.makeNot(f) for every proposition on every transition.

Fixes:
- Intern by structure, with a key of (kind, sub1.id, sub2.id) or the proposition name. That costs O(1) instead of O(size of formula).
- Also, or instead, cache derived formulas directly on the Formula in fields like negation and nextOf.

3. correctGraph rescans every edge on every round

getUnsatisfactoryEdges() walks all nodes and all edges in both directions and re-runs isSatisfactoryTowards each round. Once an atom is in the graph it doesn't change, so whether an edge is satisfactory is fixed when the edge is created. Use a worklist instead: in addEdge, if the new edge isn't satisfactory, push it onto a queue, then process the queue until it's empty. Total work drops from O(rounds × E) to O(E).

4. Store Atom as a BitSet of formula ids

Formula ids are dense integers from newId(), so a BitSet fits well. Right now Atom is a TreeSet<Formula>:
- contains costs O(log n).
- hashCode() is recomputed in O(n) on every HashMap operation in nodes, because nothing caches it.
- Copies, including the two TreeSet copies in every SplitKey, allocate a tree n
                                                                                                                                                                            With a BitSet, contains, equals, hashCode and clone all become cheap word operawo BitSets. You could also compute two more BitSets once per atom: the ids itsX-formulas require of the next state, and the ids its Y/Ỹ-formulas require of talready split. Then isSatisfactoryTowards is just a subset test(req.andNot(neighbour.members) is empty) instead of a loop of instanceof checks The same applies to getNextSet/getPrevSet, which correctEdge currently rebuilds
every time.

